    fix(board): header --set-* refuses on headerless boards instead of corrupting the first task row — line 1 shape now gates topology-B header writes and validate header checks. Addresses BT-037.
    
    MEASURED (2026-09-19, on a copy of the real /home/kara/coding-hermes-tools
    board: tasks.jsonl + events.jsonl, no board.jsonl, line 1 = task CHT-001).
    
    Pre-fix (binary built from a1e123e):
      header --set-ticks-total 90   -> exit 0, "header updated (line 1 of
                                       tasks.jsonl)"
      tasks.jsonl sha256            -> 3f41f48a... -> fe66bd86...
      CHT-001                       -> 9 keys -> 10, ticks_total:90, rewritten
                                       updated_at (the reported corruption)
      validate                      -> exit 1 with THREE false errors
                                       "tasks.jsonl header: version/ticks_total/
                                       ticks_idle is not an integer counter"
    
    Post-fix (same fixture, same command):
      exit 1, stderr: 'no header on this board: ... line 1 of tasks.jsonl is the
      TASK row "CHT-001" — a header operation there would rewrite that task row';
      tasks.jsonl sha256 identical before/after (5583768e... on the live copy) and
      the first row still has its 9 keys; validate exits 0 with one informational
      line "headerless board (no board.jsonl)" and none of the false errors.
    
    CHANGE
    - Resolve(): topology B (no board.jsonl) is only claimed when line 1 of
      tasks.jsonl is header-SHAPED (reuses rowIsHeaderShape); otherwise the board
      is HEADERLESS (Topology stays "B", HasHeader() is false). Reads are
      untouched — task enumeration already skips line 1 only when it IS a header.
    - HeaderRow()/SetHeader() refuse on a headerless board with the ErrNoHeader
      sentinel ("no header on this board", naming the task row), so the CLI exits 1
      and nothing is written. bumpHeaderTicksTotal() is a no-op there, so
      `event --tick N` still appends instead of failing on a counter it cannot own.
    - Hard guard: SetHeader additionally refuses to stamp header keys into any row
      carrying id/title/status (rowIsTaskShape) — belt and braces over the
      classification, covering a board.jsonl that holds a task row.
    - validate/doctor: one informational headerless line and the header counter /
      drift checks skipped, instead of validating a task row as the header.
    - An UNPARSEABLE line 1 is deliberately NOT classified headerless: it keeps
      surfacing the parser's own diagnosis (pretty-printed/truncated JSON) while
      the shape guard still blocks the write.
    
    VERIFICATION
    - 17 new tests (10 internal/board, 7 cmd/boardctl): headerless fixture for the
      header --set-* refusal (exit non-zero + sha256 byte-identity), the validate
      informational path, the hard-guard RED case (classification defeated, guard
      still fires), topology-A task-row guard case, reads/create/event still
      working, empty tasks.jsonl, and unparseable-line-1 not masking parse errors.
    - RED proven by disabling only the guards: pre-fix behaviour returns (SetHeader
      err=nil, sha changed, 9 -> 10 keys, validate 2 false errors; CLI: header
      --set exits 0, header --json prints the task row, validate/doctor exit 1,
      event --tick rewrites tasks.jsonl).
    - Real topology-B board (header-shaped line 1): header --set-ticks-total 7
      still exits 0 and rewrites ONLY line 1; existing topology-B tests unmoved.
      Topology-A (boardctl's own board): header --set + validate unchanged, only
      board.jsonl touched.
    - Fleet sweep on copies: coding-hermes-tools 3 false errors -> exit 0 with the
      headerless note; dexdat-memory 3 false errors -> 0 (its 26 duplicate-id
      errors are real and untouched). 4 other scan hits are partial boards (no
      events.jsonl) and do not resolve, unchanged by this fix.
    - go build ./... + go vet ./... clean; go test ./... -count=1 -short green;
      gofmt clean; gitreins guard PASS (test mode: full, go_tests passed).
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
